    "# Barabasi-Albert Graph Analysis: Real-World Graph Properties\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction to Barabasi-Albert Graphs\n",
    "\n",
    "Barabasi-Albert (BA) graphs are scale-free networks that model real-world phenomena where some nodes have many connections (hubs) while most nodes have few connections. This follows a power-law degree distribution, making them more representative of real-world networks like social networks, the internet, and biological networks.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why This Matters for Our Research\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Real-world relevance**: BA graphs better represent actual networks than random graphs\n",
    "2. **NeurIPS competitiveness**: Shows your work applies to practical scenarios\n",
    "3. **Model robustness**: Tests whether GNNs/CNNs generalize to different graph structures\n",
    "4. **Scale-free properties**: Different structural challenges than Erdős-Rényi graphs\n",
    "\n",
    "### BA Graph Parameters\n",
    "- **n**: Number of nodes\n",
    "- **m**: Number of edges to attach from a new node to existing nodes\n",
    "- **seed**: For reproducibility\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll generate BA graphs with similar node counts as our Erdős-Rényi experiments (5-64 nodes) and compare model performance.\n"
   ]
